+
+# *** 2026-11-03 (route 190): THE STOP HOLD — the one lever that reaches the CLUTCH ***
+# THE MEASUREMENT: at a standstill the driver's own pedal puts Clutch_Driving_Tq (0x162) at 0.0 Nm over 3,796 frames -
+# no torque through the clutch, i.e. functionally released - while our FCA11 hold leaves it at +26 Nm over 3,994 frames
+# (creep torque live, the "50 lb-ft at the light" the owner reported). A 7 LSB request does NOT fix it at v == 0: the
+# brake pressure the ESC builds from CR_VSM_DecCmd is injected DOWNSTREAM of the master-cylinder sensor, so the car's own
+# pressure reading stays 0.00 bar and the transmission never learns the car is being held. The clutch therefore follows
+# the PEDAL-side signal, not the caliper.
+# THE LEVER: FCA11 carries two fields we have never set - FCA_StopReq (21|1) and CF_VSM_HBACmd (1|2) - and the camera's
+# own frames are inert at a standstill (both read 0 in every logged frame), so mirroring them does nothing. While the law
+# is HOLDING the car at a stop, ask the ESC for its own stop hold with hydraulic assist: the ESC then owns the stop, which
+# is the brake event a transmission reacts to. Gated on the dynamic-brakes instance, so the deployed law never changes a
+# byte, and never CLEARS the camera's bits (None = mirror).
+FCA11_STOP_HOLD_V_KPH = 2.0               # km/h: the hold request is armed at/below this speed (the stop band)
+FCA11_STOP_HOLD_MIN_LSB = FCA11_CRAWL_MIN_LSB   # LSB: ... and only when the law is actually holding (>= the clutch-open level)
